#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-worktree(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:7
msgid "git-worktree - Manage multiple working trees"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit worktree add [-f] [--detach] [--checkout] [--lock [--reason "
"<string>]]\n"
"\t\t\t [--orphan] [(-b | -B) <new-branch>] <path> [<commit-ish>]\n"
"\tgit worktree list [-v | --porcelain [-z]]\n"
"\tgit worktree lock [--reason <string>] <worktree>\n"
"\tgit worktree move <worktree> <new-path>\n"
"\tgit worktree prune [-n] [-v] [--expire <expire>]\n"
"\tgit worktree remove [-f] <worktree>\n"
"\tgit worktree repair [<path>...]\n"
"\tgit worktree unlock <worktree>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:26
msgid "Manage multiple working trees attached to the same repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:32
msgid ""
"A git repository can support multiple working trees, allowing you to check "
"out more than one branch at a time.  With `git worktree add` a new working "
"tree is associated with the repository, along with additional metadata that "
"differentiates that working tree from others in the same repository.  The "
"working tree, along with this metadata, is called a \"worktree\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:38
msgid ""
"This new worktree is called a \"linked worktree\" as opposed to the \"main "
"worktree\" prepared by linkgit:git-init[1] or linkgit:git-clone[1].  A "
"repository has one main worktree (if it's not a bare repository) and zero or "
"more linked worktrees. When you are done with a linked worktree, remove it "
"with `git worktree remove`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:50
msgid ""
"In its simplest form, `git worktree add <path>` automatically creates a new "
"branch whose name is the final component of _<path>_, which is convenient if "
"you plan to work on a new topic. For instance, `git worktree add ../hotfix` "
"creates new branch `hotfix` and checks it out at path `../hotfix`. To "
"instead work on an existing branch in a new worktree, use `git worktree add "
"<path> <branch>`. On the other hand, if you just plan to make some "
"experimental changes or do testing without disturbing existing development, "
"it is often convenient to create a 'throwaway' worktree not associated with "
"any branch. For instance, `git worktree add -d <path>` creates a new "
"worktree with a detached `HEAD` at the same commit as the current branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:57
msgid ""
"If a working tree is deleted without using `git worktree remove`, then its "
"associated administrative files, which reside in the repository (see "
"\"DETAILS\" below), will eventually be removed automatically (see "
"`gc.worktreePruneExpire` in linkgit:git-config[1]), or you can run `git "
"worktree prune` in the main or any linked worktree to clean up any stale "
"administrative files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:63
msgid ""
"If the working tree for a linked worktree is stored on a portable device or "
"network share which is not always mounted, you can prevent its "
"administrative files from being pruned by issuing the `git worktree lock` "
"command, optionally specifying `--reason` to explain why the worktree is "
"locked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "COMMANDS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "`add <path> [<commit-ish>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:72
msgid ""
"Create a worktree at _<path>_ and checkout _<commit-ish>_ into it. The new "
"worktree is linked to the current repository, sharing everything except "
"per-worktree files such as `HEAD`, `index`, etc. As a convenience, "
"_<commit-ish>_ may be a bare \"`-`\", which is synonymous with `@{-1}`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:77
msgid ""
"If _<commit-ish>_ is a branch name (call it _<branch>_) and is not found, "
"and neither `-b` nor `-B` nor `--detach` are used, but there does exist a "
"tracking branch in exactly one remote (call it _<remote>_)  with a matching "
"name, treat as equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git worktree add --track -b <branch> <path> <remote>/<branch>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:90
msgid ""
"If the branch exists in multiple remotes and one of them is named by the "
"`checkout.defaultRemote` configuration variable, we'll use that one for the "
"purposes of disambiguation, even if the _<branch>_ isn't unique across all "
"remotes. Set it to e.g. `checkout.defaultRemote=origin` to always checkout "
"remote branches from there if _<branch>_ is ambiguous but exists on the "
"`origin` remote. See also `checkout.defaultRemote` in linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:98
msgid ""
"If _<commit-ish>_ is omitted and neither `-b` nor `-B` nor `--detach` used, "
"then, as a convenience, the new worktree is associated with a branch (call "
"it _<branch>_) named after `$(basename <path>)`.  If _<branch>_ doesn't "
"exist, a new branch based on `HEAD` is automatically created as if `-b "
"<branch>` was given.  If _<branch>_ does exist, it will be checked out in "
"the new worktree, if it's not checked out anywhere else, otherwise the "
"command will refuse to create the worktree (unless `--force` is used)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:108
msgid ""
"If _<commit-ish>_ is omitted, neither `--detach`, or `--orphan` is used, and "
"there are no valid local branches (or remote branches if `--guess-remote` is "
"specified) then, as a convenience, the new worktree is associated with a new "
"unborn branch named _<branch>_ (after `$(basename <path>)` if neither `-b` "
"or `-B` is used) as if `--orphan` was passed to the command. In the event "
"the repository has a remote and `--guess-remote` is used, but no remote or "
"local branches exist, then the command fails with a warning reminding the "
"user to fetch from their remote first (or override by using `-f/--force`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "`list`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:117
msgid ""
"List details of each worktree.  The main worktree is listed first, followed "
"by each of the linked worktrees.  The output details include whether the "
"worktree is bare, the revision currently checked out, the branch currently "
"checked out (or \"detached HEAD\" if none), \"locked\" if the worktree is "
"locked, \"prunable\" if the worktree can be pruned by the `prune` command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "`lock`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:124
msgid ""
"If a worktree is on a portable device or network share which is not always "
"mounted, lock it to prevent its administrative files from being pruned "
"automatically. This also prevents it from being moved or deleted.  "
"Optionally, specify a reason for the lock with `--reason`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "`move`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:131
msgid ""
"Move a worktree to a new location. Note that the main worktree or linked "
"worktrees containing submodules cannot be moved with this command. (The `git "
"worktree repair` command, however, can reestablish the connection with "
"linked worktrees if you move the main worktree manually.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "`prune`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:135
msgid "Prune worktree information in `$GIT_DIR/worktrees`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:136
#, no-wrap
msgid "`remove`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:142
msgid ""
"Remove a worktree. Only clean worktrees (no untracked files and no "
"modification in tracked files) can be removed. Unclean worktrees or ones "
"with submodules can be removed with `--force`. The main worktree cannot be "
"removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "`repair [<path>...]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:147
msgid ""
"Repair worktree administrative files, if possible, if they have become "
"corrupted or outdated due to external factors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:152
msgid ""
"For instance, if the main worktree (or bare repository) is moved, linked "
"worktrees will be unable to locate it. Running `repair` in the main worktree "
"will reestablish the connection from linked worktrees back to the main "
"worktree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:159
msgid ""
"Similarly, if the working tree for a linked worktree is moved without using "
"`git worktree move`, the main worktree (or bare repository) will be unable "
"to locate it. Running `repair` within the recently-moved worktree will "
"reestablish the connection. If multiple linked worktrees are moved, running "
"`repair` from any worktree with each tree's new _<path>_ as an argument, "
"will reestablish the connection to all the specified paths."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:164
msgid ""
"If both the main worktree and linked worktrees have been moved or copied "
"manually, then running `repair` in the main worktree and specifying the new "
"_<path>_ of each linked worktree will reestablish all connections in both "
"directions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "`unlock`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:168
msgid "Unlock a worktree, allowing it to be pruned, moved or deleted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-f`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:180
msgid ""
"By default, `add` refuses to create a new worktree when _<commit-ish>_ is a "
"branch name and is already checked out by another worktree, or if _<path>_ "
"is already assigned to some worktree but is missing (for instance, if "
"_<path>_ was deleted manually). This option overrides these safeguards. To "
"add a missing but locked worktree path, specify `--force` twice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:185
msgid ""
"`move` refuses to move a locked worktree unless `--force` is specified "
"twice. If the destination is already assigned to some other worktree but is "
"missing (for instance, if _<new-path>_ was deleted manually), then `--force` "
"allows the move to proceed; use `--force` twice if the destination is "
"locked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:188
msgid ""
"`remove` refuses to remove an unclean worktree unless `--force` is used.  To "
"remove a locked worktree, specify `--force` twice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:189
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-b <new-branch>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:190
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-B <new-branch>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:197
msgid ""
"With `add`, create a new branch named _<new-branch>_ starting at "
"_<commit-ish>_, and check out _<new-branch>_ into the new worktree.  If "
"_<commit-ish>_ is omitted, it defaults to `HEAD`.  By default, `-b` refuses "
"to create a new branch if it already exists. `-B` overrides this safeguard, "
"resetting _<new-branch>_ to _<commit-ish>_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:198
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-d`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--detach`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:202
msgid ""
"With `add`, detach `HEAD` in the new worktree. See \"DETACHED HEAD\" in "
"linkgit:git-checkout[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--checkout`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:204
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-checkout`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:209
msgid ""
"By default, `add` checks out _<commit-ish>_, however, `--no-checkout` can be "
"used to suppress checkout in order to make customizations, such as "
"configuring sparse-checkout. See \"Sparse checkout\" in "
"linkgit:git-read-tree[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:210
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--guess-remote`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:211
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-guess-remote`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:217
msgid ""
"With `worktree add <path>`, without _<commit-ish>_, instead of creating a "
"new branch from `HEAD`, if there exists a tracking branch in exactly one "
"remote matching the basename of _<path>_, base the new branch on the "
"remote-tracking branch, and mark the remote-tracking branch as \"upstream\" "
"from the new branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:220
msgid ""
"This can also be set up as the default behaviour by using the "
"`worktree.guessRemote` config option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:221
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--relative-paths`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-relative-paths`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:226
msgid ""
"Link worktrees using relative paths or absolute paths (default).  Overrides "
"the `worktree.useRelativePaths` config option, see linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:229
msgid ""
"With `repair`, the linking files will be updated if there's an "
"absolute/relative mismatch, even if the links are correct."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:230
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--track`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:231
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-track`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:236
msgid ""
"When creating a new branch, if _<commit-ish>_ is a branch, mark it as "
"\"upstream\" from the new branch.  This is the default if _<commit-ish>_ is "
"a remote-tracking branch.  See `--track` in linkgit:git-branch[1] for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:237
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--lock`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:241
msgid ""
"Keep the worktree locked after creation. This is the equivalent of `git "
"worktree lock` after `git worktree add`, but without a race condition."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:242
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:243
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--dry-run`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:246
msgid "With `prune`, do not remove anything; just report what it would remove."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:247
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--orphan`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:250
msgid ""
"With `add`, make the new worktree and index empty, associating the worktree "
"with a new unborn branch named _<new-branch>_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:251
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--porcelain`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:256
msgid ""
"With `list`, output in an easy-to-parse format for scripts.  This format "
"will remain stable across Git versions and regardless of user "
"configuration.  It is recommended to combine this with `-z`.  See below for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:257
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-z`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:262
msgid ""
"Terminate each line with a _NUL_ rather than a newline when `--porcelain` is "
"specified with `list`. This makes it possible to parse the output when a "
"worktree path contains a newline character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:263
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:264
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:266
msgid "With `add`, suppress feedback messages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:267
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-v`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:268
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:270
msgid "With `prune`, report all removals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:272
msgid "With `list`, output additional information about worktrees (see below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:273
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--expire <time>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:275
msgid "With `prune`, only expire unused worktrees older than _<time>_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:278
msgid ""
"With `list`, annotate missing worktrees as prunable if they are older than "
"_<time>_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:279
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reason <string>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:282
msgid "With `lock` or with `add --lock`, an explanation why the worktree is locked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:283
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<worktree>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:285
msgid "Worktrees can be identified by path, either relative or absolute."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:290
msgid ""
"If the last path components in the worktree's path is unique among "
"worktrees, it can be used to identify a worktree. For example if you only "
"have two worktrees, at `/abc/def/ghi` and `/abc/def/ggg`, then `ghi` or "
"`def/ghi` is enough to point to the former worktree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:292
#, no-wrap
msgid "REFS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:297
msgid ""
"When using multiple worktrees, some refs are shared between all worktrees, "
"but others are specific to an individual worktree. One example is `HEAD`, "
"which is different for each worktree. This section is about the sharing "
"rules and how to access refs of one worktree from another."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:303
msgid ""
"In general, all pseudo refs are per-worktree and all refs starting with "
"`refs/` are shared. Pseudo refs are ones like `HEAD` which are directly "
"under `$GIT_DIR` instead of inside `$GIT_DIR/refs`. There are exceptions, "
"however: refs inside `refs/bisect`, `refs/worktree` and `refs/rewritten` are "
"not shared."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:308
msgid ""
"Refs that are per-worktree can still be accessed from another worktree via "
"two special paths, `main-worktree` and `worktrees`. The former gives access "
"to per-worktree refs of the main worktree, while the latter to all linked "
"worktrees."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:315
msgid ""
"For example, `main-worktree/HEAD` or `main-worktree/refs/bisect/good` "
"resolve to the same value as the main worktree's `HEAD` and "
"`refs/bisect/good` respectively. Similarly, `worktrees/foo/HEAD` or "
"`worktrees/bar/refs/bisect/bad` are the same as "
"`$GIT_COMMON_DIR/worktrees/foo/HEAD` and "
"`$GIT_COMMON_DIR/worktrees/bar/refs/bisect/bad`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:319
msgid ""
"To access refs, it's best not to look inside `$GIT_DIR` directly. Instead "
"use commands such as linkgit:git-rev-parse[1] or linkgit:git-update-ref[1] "
"which will handle refs correctly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:321
#, no-wrap
msgid "CONFIGURATION FILE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:326
msgid ""
"By default, the repository `config` file is shared across all worktrees.  If "
"the config variables `core.bare` or `core.worktree` are present in the "
"common config file and `extensions.worktreeConfig` is disabled, then they "
"will be applied to the main worktree only."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:329
msgid ""
"In order to have worktree-specific configuration, you can turn on the "
"`worktreeConfig` extension, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:332
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git config extensions.worktreeConfig true\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:338
msgid ""
"In this mode, specific configuration stays in the path pointed by `git "
"rev-parse --git-path config.worktree`. You can add or update configuration "
"in this file with `git config --worktree`. Older Git versions will refuse to "
"access repositories with this extension."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:344
msgid ""
"Note that in this file, the exception for `core.bare` and `core.worktree` is "
"gone. If they exist in `$GIT_DIR/config`, you must move them to the "
"`config.worktree` of the main worktree. You may also take this opportunity "
"to review and move other configuration that you do not want to share to all "
"worktrees:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:346
msgid "`core.worktree` should never be shared."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:348
msgid "`core.bare` should not be shared if the value is `core.bare=true`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:351
msgid ""
"`core.sparseCheckout` should not be shared, unless you are sure you always "
"use sparse checkout for all worktrees."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:354
msgid ""
"See the documentation of `extensions.worktreeConfig` in "
"linkgit:git-config[1] for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:356
#, no-wrap
msgid "DETAILS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:365
msgid ""
"Each linked worktree has a private sub-directory in the repository's "
"`$GIT_DIR/worktrees` directory.  The private sub-directory's name is usually "
"the base name of the linked worktree's path, possibly appended with a number "
"to make it unique.  For example, when `$GIT_DIR=/path/main/.git` the command "
"`git worktree add /path/other/test-next next` creates the linked worktree in "
"`/path/other/test-next` and also creates a `$GIT_DIR/worktrees/test-next` "
"directory (or `$GIT_DIR/worktrees/test-next1` if `test-next` is already "
"taken)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:371
msgid ""
"Within a linked worktree, `$GIT_DIR` is set to point to this private "
"directory (e.g. `/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next` in the example) and "
"`$GIT_COMMON_DIR` is set to point back to the main worktree's `$GIT_DIR` "
"(e.g. `/path/main/.git`). These settings are made in a `.git` file located "
"at the top directory of the linked worktree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:381
msgid ""
"Path resolution via `git rev-parse --git-path` uses either `$GIT_DIR` or "
"`$GIT_COMMON_DIR` depending on the path. For example, in the linked worktree "
"`git rev-parse --git-path HEAD` returns "
"`/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next/HEAD` (not "
"`/path/other/test-next/.git/HEAD` or `/path/main/.git/HEAD`) while `git "
"rev-parse --git-path refs/heads/master` uses `$GIT_COMMON_DIR` and returns "
"`/path/main/.git/refs/heads/master`, since refs are shared across all "
"worktrees, except `refs/bisect`, `refs/worktree` and `refs/rewritten`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:386
msgid ""
"See linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5] for more information. The rule of thumb "
"is do not make any assumption about whether a path belongs to `$GIT_DIR` or "
"`$GIT_COMMON_DIR` when you need to directly access something inside "
"`$GIT_DIR`. Use `git rev-parse --git-path` to get the final path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:394
msgid ""
"If you manually move a linked worktree, you need to update the `gitdir` file "
"in the entry's directory. For example, if a linked worktree is moved to "
"`/newpath/test-next` and its `.git` file points to "
"`/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next`, then update "
"`/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next/gitdir` to reference "
"`/newpath/test-next` instead. Better yet, run `git worktree repair` to "
"reestablish the connection automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:405
msgid ""
"To prevent a `$GIT_DIR/worktrees` entry from being pruned (which can be "
"useful in some situations, such as when the entry's worktree is stored on a "
"portable device), use the `git worktree lock` command, which adds a file "
"named `locked` to the entry's directory. The file contains the reason in "
"plain text. For example, if a linked worktree's `.git` file points to "
"`/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next` then a file named "
"`/path/main/.git/worktrees/test-next/locked` will prevent the `test-next` "
"entry from being pruned.  See linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:408
msgid ""
"When `extensions.worktreeConfig` is enabled, the config file "
"`.git/worktrees/<id>/config.worktree` is read after `.git/config` is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:410
#, no-wrap
msgid "LIST OUTPUT FORMAT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:413
msgid ""
"The `worktree list` command has two output formats. The default format shows "
"the details on a single line with columns.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree list\n"
"/path/to/bare-source            (bare)\n"
"/path/to/linked-worktree        abcd1234 [master]\n"
"/path/to/other-linked-worktree  1234abc  (detached HEAD)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:423
msgid ""
"The command also shows annotations for each worktree, according to its "
"state.  These annotations are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:425
msgid "`locked`, if the worktree is locked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:426
msgid "`prunable`, if the worktree can be pruned via `git worktree prune`."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree list\n"
"/path/to/linked-worktree    abcd1234 [master]\n"
"/path/to/locked-worktree    acbd5678 (brancha) locked\n"
"/path/to/prunable-worktree  5678abc  (detached HEAD) prunable\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:437
msgid ""
"For these annotations, a reason might also be available and this can be seen "
"using the verbose mode. The annotation is then moved to the next line "
"indented followed by the additional information."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:446
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree list --verbose\n"
"/path/to/linked-worktree              abcd1234 [master]\n"
"/path/to/locked-worktree-no-reason    abcd5678 (detached HEAD) locked\n"
"/path/to/locked-worktree-with-reason  1234abcd (brancha)\n"
"\tlocked: worktree path is mounted on a portable device\n"
"/path/to/prunable-worktree            5678abc1 (detached HEAD)\n"
"\tprunable: gitdir file points to non-existent location\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:451
msgid ""
"Note that the annotation is moved to the next line if the additional "
"information is available, otherwise it stays on the same line as the "
"worktree itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:453
#, no-wrap
msgid "Porcelain Format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:462
msgid ""
"The porcelain format has a line per attribute.  If `-z` is given then the "
"lines are terminated with NUL rather than a newline.  Attributes are listed "
"with a label and value separated by a single space.  Boolean attributes "
"(like `bare` and `detached`) are listed as a label only, and are present "
"only if the value is true.  Some attributes (like `locked`) can be listed as "
"a label only or with a value depending upon whether a reason is available.  "
"The first attribute of a worktree is always `worktree`, an empty line "
"indicates the end of the record.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:467
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree list --porcelain\n"
"worktree /path/to/bare-source\n"
"bare\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:471
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"worktree /path/to/linked-worktree\n"
"HEAD abcd1234abcd1234abcd1234abcd1234abcd1234\n"
"branch refs/heads/master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:475
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"worktree /path/to/other-linked-worktree\n"
"HEAD 1234abc1234abc1234abc1234abc1234abc1234a\n"
"detached\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:480
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"worktree /path/to/linked-worktree-locked-no-reason\n"
"HEAD 5678abc5678abc5678abc5678abc5678abc5678c\n"
"branch refs/heads/locked-no-reason\n"
"locked\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:485
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"worktree /path/to/linked-worktree-locked-with-reason\n"
"HEAD 3456def3456def3456def3456def3456def3456b\n"
"branch refs/heads/locked-with-reason\n"
"locked reason why is locked\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:490
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"worktree /path/to/linked-worktree-prunable\n"
"HEAD 1233def1234def1234def1234def1234def1234b\n"
"detached\n"
"prunable gitdir file points to non-existent location\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:497
msgid ""
"Unless `-z` is used any \"unusual\" characters in the lock reason such as "
"newlines are escaped and the entire reason is quoted as explained for the "
"configuration variable `core.quotePath` (see linkgit:git-config[1]).  For "
"Example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree list --porcelain\n"
"...\n"
"locked \"reason\\nwhy is locked\"\n"
"...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:506
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:515
msgid ""
"You are in the middle of a refactoring session and your boss comes in and "
"demands that you fix something immediately. You might typically use "
"linkgit:git-stash[1] to store your changes away temporarily, however, your "
"working tree is in such a state of disarray (with new, moved, and removed "
"files, and other bits and pieces strewn around) that you don't want to risk "
"disturbing any of it. Instead, you create a temporary linked worktree to "
"make the emergency fix, remove it when done, and then resume your earlier "
"refactoring session."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:523
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git worktree add -b emergency-fix ../temp master\n"
"$ pushd ../temp\n"
"# ... hack hack hack ...\n"
"$ git commit -a -m 'emergency fix for boss'\n"
"$ popd\n"
"$ git worktree remove ../temp\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:526
#, no-wrap
msgid "CONFIGURATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:533
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUGS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-worktree.adoc:537
msgid ""
"Multiple checkout in general is still experimental, and the support for "
"submodules is incomplete. It is NOT recommended to make multiple checkouts "
"of a superproject."
msgstr ""

